/**
 * dbml-sharepoint PROBE: BUILT-IN PERMISSION LEVELS, EDITABILITY CENSUS
 *
 * QUESTION: which of SharePoint Online's BUILT-IN permission levels can a
 * site owner edit, delete, impersonate or shadow through the REST API, and
 * does the platform tell you when it refuses?
 *
 * WHY. On 2026-09-06 `read-level-tamper-probe.js` MERGEd BasePermissions on
 * the BUILT-IN Read level (RoleTypeKind 2, Id 1073741826) of a live site and
 * SharePoint Online accepted it: HTTP 204, reading back High=176
 * Low=138612832 with ViewListItems gone. The level went on being called Read.
 * The deploy's phase 1.6 bitmap verifier refused that site, which is recorded
 * for Read alone in `src/dbml_sharepoint/templates/deploy/_reader_enrolment.js.j2`.
 * One level was measured; the guard is written as though the answer holds for
 * all of them. This probe asks the same question of every built-in level, and
 * asks the four adjacent questions a reader of that finding immediately has:
 * can they be deleted, can a custom level wear a built-in RoleTypeKind, can a
 * custom level wear a built-in NAME, and does clearing one bit move others.
 *
 * WHAT MICROSOFT DOCUMENTS (the research arm, question 1)
 *
 * Create and edit permission levels
 *   https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sharepoint/how-to-create-and-edit-permission-levels
 *   (ms.date 2026-05-07, page updated 2026-07-01)
 *
 *   "Don't change the default permission levels that come with SharePoint.
 *    You can't change the Full Control and Limited Access default permission
 *    levels."
 *
 *   "You can delete any permission level, except for Full Control and Limited
 *    Access. Don't delete a default permission level."
 *
 *   "Don't edit default permission levels If you change a default permission
 *    level, your changes affect all the built-in SharePoint groups within the
 *    site collection."
 *
 * Understanding permission levels in SharePoint
 *   https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sharepoint/understanding-permission-levels
 *   (ms.date 2025-04-30)
 *
 *   "You can change any of the default permission levels, except Full Control
 *    and Limited Access."
 *
 *   Of Full Control: "Contains all available SharePoint permissions... You
 *   can't customize or delete it."
 *
 *   And the dependency cascade this probe's question 3 is about: "When you
 *   select a SharePoint permission that depends on another, SharePoint
 *   automatically selects the associated permission. Similarly, when you clear
 *   SharePoint permission, SharePoint automatically clears any SharePoint
 *   permission that depends on it. For example, when you clear View Items,
 *   SharePoint automatically clears Manage Lists"; and "The only SharePoint
 *   permission without a dependency is Open."
 *
 * User permissions and permission levels in SharePoint Server
 *   https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sharepoint/sites/user-permissions-and-permission-levels
 *   (APPLIES TO includes SharePoint in Microsoft 365)
 *
 *   "You can customize the permissions contained within five of these
 *    permission levels. You can't customize the permissions within the Limited
 *    Access and Full Control permission levels."
 *
 *   "Limited Access can't be edited or deleted."
 *
 * RoleType Enum (Microsoft.SharePoint.Client)
 *   https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/microsoft.sharepoint.client.roletype
 *
 *   None 0, Guest 1, Reader 2, Contributor 3, WebDesigner 4, Administrator 5,
 *   Editor 6, Reviewer 7, RestrictedReader 8, System 255. This probe keys its
 *   roster on RoleTypeKind rather than on Name, because the built-in names are
 *   English and a level's Name is writable, which is what question 6 is about.
 *
 * So the documentation sorts into three piles, and the piles are what the run
 * is compared against:
 *
 *   documented-as-impossible   editing or deleting Full Control, and editing
 *                              or deleting Limited Access.
 *   documented-as-discouraged  editing or deleting every other built-in. Both
 *                              are explicitly permitted and advised against,
 *                              because the change reaches the whole site
 *                              collection.
 *   undocumented               whether the REST surface enforces any of it;
 *                              whether the UI's dependency cascade also
 *                              applies to a REST MERGE (the 2026-09-06 tamper
 *                              moved exactly one bit and nothing cascaded,
 *                              which is what question 3 generalises); whether
 *                              RoleTypeKind is server-assigned on create; and
 *                              whether two levels on one site may share a name.
 *
 * Nothing below is asserted from that documentation. It is recorded as a
 * PREDICTION and `documented-editability-agrees` reports where the run and the
 * documentation disagree, which is the only form in which a Learn sentence is
 * evidence about a tenant.
 *
 * WHAT IT ASKS. Ids follow the grammar in `test/manual/SURFACES.md`:
 * `<surface>.<scope>.<question>`. All of them file under `access` on the
 * `built-in-level` scope, which this probe adds.
 *
 *   CONTROLS
 *   control-roledefinitions-readable    can this caller enumerate
 *                                       web/roledefinitions with their
 *                                       BasePermissions at all?
 *   control-roster-complete             is every level this probe targets
 *                                       present exactly once, keyed by
 *                                       RoleTypeKind?
 *   control-read-mask-matches-reference does this site's built-in Read still
 *                                       carry the mask measured on
 *                                       2026-08-14? A site that arrives
 *                                       tampered stops the write arm here.
 *
 *   CENSUS AND DOCUMENTATION
 *   level-census                        Id, Name, RoleTypeKind and both
 *                                       bitmap halves for every level on the
 *                                       site, read BEFORE any write.
 *   documented-editability-agrees       does the run agree with what Learn
 *                                       documents, level by level?
 *
 *   PER LEVEL, safest first. <slug> is guest, reader, contributor, editor,
 *   webdesigner, administrator, from RoleTypeKind 1, 2, 3, 6, 4, 5.
 *   <slug>-basepermissions-editable     does a one-bit MERGE stick?
 *   <slug>-no-silent-cascade            while that bit was cleared, did any
 *                                       OTHER bit change?
 *   <slug>-restore-verified             did the pre-write mask read back
 *                                       exactly?
 *   <slug>-deletable                    is DELETE refused, and with what?
 *
 *   MASQUERADE (question 5)
 *   masquerade-create-accepted          is a POST carrying RoleTypeKind 2
 *                                       accepted for a custom level?
 *   masquerade-roletypekind-readback    does the read-back keep 2, or is it
 *                                       forced to 0?
 *   masquerade-delete-guarded           is DELETE refused for a custom level
 *                                       wearing a built-in RoleTypeKind? The
 *                                       non-destructive proxy for question 4.
 *   masquerade-level-removed            was the probe's own level removed?
 *
 *   RENAME (question 6)
 *   rename-to-builtin-accepted          may a custom level take the built-in
 *                                       Read level's exact Name?
 *   duplicate-name-resolution           with a duplicate present, which Id
 *                                       does getbyname() return?
 *   rename-level-removed                was the probe's own level removed?
 *
 * THE SAFETY CONTRACT, and why each clause is in the code rather than here.
 *
 * 1. CONFIRMED and ALLOW_WRITES are both required, as for every write probe.
 *    ALLOW_DESTRUCTIVE_DELETE is a third gate; see THE DELETE ARM below.
 *
 * 2. Every level is fingerprinted (Id, Name, RoleTypeKind, both halves) in one
 *    read BEFORE any write, and the restore target for each level is that
 *    level's own PRE-WRITE mask. No constant in this file is ever written to a
 *    live site. A remembered mask is a guess about somebody else's tenant.
 *
 * 3. The write arm refuses outright if this site's built-in Read is not at the
 *    mask `reader-bindings-probe.js` measured on 2026-08-14. A site that
 *    arrives tampered must not be written to and "restored", because the
 *    restore would read back clean and the transcript would look like a clean
 *    run. Deviation is REPORTED and never repaired. The residual is stated
 *    plainly: a site whose Read was legitimately customised, which Learn
 *    permits, will refuse here too, and that is the correct trade for an
 *    object class where the wrong guess is a security change.
 *
 *    Read is the only level with a reference mask, because it is the only one
 *    this project has measured. `level-census` prints the other five so that
 *    the next run has something to compare against.
 *
 * 4. WHICH BIT IS CLEARED is a rule, not a judgement call, so a transcript can
 *    be re-derived from this file: the highest-value NAMED bit that is SET in
 *    that level's own pre-write mask, lives in the LOW half, and is not on the
 *    protected list. Unnamed bits are excluded because clearing a bit whose
 *    meaning this project cannot state is exactly what it forbids elsewhere.
 *    The LOW-half restriction is universal rather than special-cased for Full
 *    Control: two of the five protected bits live in the high half, and one
 *    rule is easier to check than two. For Full Control, whose low half is all
 *    ones, the rule selects ManageWeb, whose absence is visible in the
 *    read-back and harmless on a disposable site for the seconds it is gone,
 *    because a site collection administrator's authority is not a role
 *    definition. The protected list and its reasons are beside the code, and
 *    the rule is checked against a full mask BEFORE the first network call.
 *
 * 5. Restore is by exact read-back equality, never by the write's status code.
 *    A failed restore stops every remaining arm, prints a FAIL banner naming
 *    the Id and the exact halves to write back by hand, and is returned as a
 *    non-empty `restoreFailures` with `restored: false`. That is this file's
 *    reading of "a non-zero exit": a console probe has no process to exit.
 *
 * 6. All transport is `fetchWithRetry` and `apiUrl` from the shared core. The
 *    probe writes to the site it was pasted into and carries no SITE_URL.
 *
 * 7. Cleanup deletes the two levels this probe created and nothing else, by
 *    Id, in a `finally`, and verifies removal by re-enumeration.
 *
 * THE DELETE ARM (question 4) is behind its own flag, and both halves of the
 * roster are behind the same one. Learn documents four of the six as deletable
 * and two as not, so for four levels this is not an experiment in capturing a
 * refusal, it is a request to delete a built-in permission level from a live
 * site collection. The two documented as refused are behind the same flag
 * anyway: a probe that trusts a Learn sentence for its safety guard is the
 * exact failure mode this file exists to measure.
 *
 * The arm is VOID, flag or no flag, unless `masquerade-roletypekind-readback`
 * has already shown that a level can be created carrying a built-in
 * RoleTypeKind. If it cannot, a successful delete is unrecoverable, and this
 * probe does not ask a question whose "yes" it cannot undo. When it does run,
 * a successful delete is followed immediately by a rebuild from the
 * fingerprint. Note what a rebuild does NOT restore: the original Id, and
 * every role assignment naming that level at web, list and item scope. No
 * partial capture of those is attempted, because a partial capture would be
 * read as a backup. Run this arm on a disposable site only.
 *
 * HOW TO RUN
 *   1. Open a disposable SharePoint Online site you own.
 *   2. F12 -> Console -> paste -> Enter. The committed defaults only print.
 *   3. Set CONFIRMED and ALLOW_WRITES to true. Leave ALLOW_DESTRUCTIVE_DELETE
 *      false unless the site is genuinely disposable.
 *   4. Paste again and copy the complete results table back verbatim.
 *
 * STATUS: NOT YET RUN. Every row is open until a live transcript fills it.
 */
